The culprit is `sync_io_in_hotpath` in `loadPlanSchema`. Its evidence is:

> “`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  
> “`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  
> “`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  
> “lines: 12, 13, 15”  
> “swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it”

The blocking methods are `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`. Each blocks the Node.js event loop on every request.

The smallest migration is to make `loadPlanSchema` async, use `node:fs/promises`, and await it inside the existing async production handler:

```ts
import { readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

async function loadPlanSchema(path: string) {
  try {
    const fileStat = await stat(path);
    const contents = await readFile(path, "utf8");

    // Preserve the existing returned value and logic.
    return { fileStat, contents };
  } catch (error) {
    if (
      error instanceof Error &&
      "code" in error &&
      error.code === "ENOENT"
    ) {
      // Preserve the existing “file does not exist” result.
      return null;
    }

    throw error;
  }
}
```

Then change only the internal call site:

```ts
const schema = await loadPlanSchema(schemaPath);
```

`stat()` replaces both `existsSync()` and `statSync()`—an `ENOENT` error represents nonexistence—while `readFile()` replaces `readFileSync()`. The exported handler can retain its existing parameters and `Promise` return type, so its public signature remains unchanged.

If `loadPlanSchema` itself is publicly required to return a non-Promise value, that exact signature cannot be preserved while performing uncached filesystem I/O asynchronously; the async boundary must move to or already exist at the handler.
